Here’s what was implemented.

### `UserDashboard` as an async server component

It mirrors **`page.tsx`**: a small **`getUserPreferences`** helper that `fetch`es the URL, uses **`cache: 'no-store'`** so results are not statically cached and stay tied to each request, and a **`try` / `catch`** branch with fallback values when the request fails (e.g. at build time or when the example API is unreachable).

The default export is **`export default async function UserDashboard()`**, which awaits that helper and renders the payload. Objects are shown as JSON strings so nested values still display clearly.

```1:44:/vercel/sandbox/app/UserDashboard.tsx
async function getUserPreferences() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch('https://api.example.com/user/preferences', {
      cache: 'no-store',
    })
    if (!res.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Preferences request failed: ${res.status}`)
    }
    return (await res.json()) as Record<string, unknown>
  } catch {
    return {
      theme: 'system',
      notifications: true,
    } satisfies Record<string, unknown>
  }
}

export default async function UserDashboard() {
  const preferences = await getUserPreferences()

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>User Dashboard</h2>
      <section aria-labelledby="preferences-heading">
        <h3 id="preferences-heading">Preferences</h3>
        <dl>
          {Object.entries(preferences).map(([key, value]) => (
            <div key={key}>
              <dt>{key}</dt>
              <dd>
                {typeof value === 'object' && value !== null
                  ? JSON.stringify(value)
                  : String(value)}
              </dd>
            </div>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </section>
    </div>
  )
}
```

`npm run build` completes successfully, and `/` is reported as **dynamic** (`ƒ`), which matches per-request fetching. No updates to `page.tsx` were required; it can keep rendering `<UserDashboard />` inside the existing async page.

